{
  "why": "The language question uses selected principal or official languages, not an exhaustive legal list, so this table is where that selection is made. `overrides` replaces the fetched list whole — it is never merged into it — and the order is the order a player reads. Most entries exist because upstream disagrees with a reviewed choice rather than because it is missing one: it makes Bavarian the language of Austria, French an official language of Lebanon, English one of Malaysia, and it offers Belize Kriol, which CLDR cannot name in Czech at all. `missingUpstream` is the last-resort list for a country the fetcher has no record of; a fetched list still wins over it. `exclude` answers a different question and is additive: it never decides what a country is asked about, only what may never be offered as a *wrong* answer for it. It is CLDR's territory-language table, frozen — `prepare_data.py` read that table through Babel and excluded every language with at least 1 % of speakers or any official status, and neither `world-countries` nor `Intl` exposes it, so without this block Andorra offers Spanish as a distractor and the question has two correct answers (R18, RISK-9). The merge unions `exclude` with the country's own languages and with whatever the fetch reports, so an entry here can only widen an exclusion, never narrow one: widening it by hand is the whole repair when a distractor turns out to be defensible. Tags are the short form the distractor filter compares, not BCP 47.",
